course: dictionary
lesson: 3983
title: Galaxy, a word for the sky and for a crowd
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A galaxy is a huge group of billions of stars, gas and dust, all held together by gravity. Gravity is the pull that keeps things from floating away. Our galaxy is called the Milky Way, and «The Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy, the Milky Way.»
explain: Scientists use a telescope to see galaxies, even ones very far away. «Galileo's homemade telescope showed moons going round Jupiter» over 400 years ago, and today giant mirrors and dishes see galaxies near the edge of the visible universe.
explain: Galaxy can also be used in a looser way. «A galaxy of» means a large group of famous or bright people or things. If you read «a galaxy of stars on the stage», you can guess it means lots of famous people, not planets in space. Use the clues around the word to decide which meaning fits.
fact: A galaxy is a huge group of billions of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity.
fact: Our galaxy is the Milky Way, and the Sun is one of its stars.
fact: Galaxies can be spiral, elliptical or irregular in shape.
fact: On a dark night away from city lights, the Milky Way looks like a pale band across the sky.
fact: «A galaxy of» is an informal phrase meaning a large group of famous or bright people or things.
exercise: What is a galaxy? | A small cloud that floats near the Earth | A tiny planet with one moon | *A huge group of billions of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity
exercise: «Our galaxy is called the Milky Way, and the Sun is one of its stars.» What is the name of our galaxy? | The Sun | Andromeda | *The Milky Way | Jupiter
exercise: «The concert had a galaxy of famous singers on stage.» What does a galaxy of mean here? | A very dark, empty space | *A large group of famous or bright people | A new kind of telescope | A long journey to the stars
test: On a dark night away from city lights, what does the Milky Way look like? | A row of city lamps | A red spot on the ground | A round white ball | *A pale band across the sky
test: «Galileo's homemade telescope showed moons going round which planet?» | Earth | Mars | Venus | *Jupiter
exam: Which three shapes can scientists use to name a galaxy? | Hot, cold or warm | Big, small or medium | Round, square or triangular | *Spiral, elliptical or irregular
